ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066143 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab4ce6ab-f572-536f-b69b-26107e70c78f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066144 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066144 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066144 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1c0a61e3-aadd-51f2-af52-df568c7fa6e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066145 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066145 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066145 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "48a7fd8b-fc8e-504d-805e-8ece07aca323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066149 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066149 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066149 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcca67ee-d00b-5ed9-a1a5-a17cc2e60ae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066150 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066150 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066150 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "33b677ae-2eee-5574-a670-188edbba3875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066151 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066151 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066151 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "503e8508-9d51-5534-9ba4-e03eddaf3997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068491 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068491 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\left\\vert\\dfrac{x-1}{6}\\right\\vert \\leq 1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60c2e025-0b7e-5ac2-afec-adeb5f65eed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068491 #edb/ref 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value.\n\nThe inequality specifies every number\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{6}\n$$\n\nwhose distance from zero is less than or equal to $1$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\dfrac{x-1}{6} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\le \\frac{x - 1}{6} \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le x - 1 \\le 6 \\\\ -5 & \\le x \\le 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068493 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068493 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\mid 12x - 5 \\mid \\ge 7$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8497ea21-c194-5bbf-b501-a0e9749d8dc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068493 #edb/ref 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value.\n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12x - 5$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $7$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n12x - 5\\begin{vmatrix}\\le - 7 & or & 12x - 5\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 7\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 5 & \\le - 7 & 12x - 5 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 12x & \\le - 2 & 12x & \\ge 12 \\\\ x & \\le - \\frac{2}{12} & x & \\ge \\frac{12}{12} \\\\ x & \\le - \\frac{1}{6} & x & \\ge 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\nx\\begin{vmatrix}\\le - \\frac{1}{6} & or & x\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068510 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068510 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\vert 10 - 2x \\vert +1 < 5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12d8aea9-62dd-5181-bd6e-54c11eba4152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068510 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}10 - 2x & + 1\\end{vmatrix}< 5; \\mid 10 - 2x \\mid \\mid < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value.\n\nThe inequality specifies every number $10-2x$ whose distance from zero is less than $4$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 < 10 - 2x < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 < 10 - 2x & < 4 \\\\ -14 < - 2x & < - 6 \\\\ 14 > 2x & > 6 \\\\ 7 > x & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3 < x < 7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1039 "Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Less Than (or Equal To) Symbol" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068717 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e107f878-5b22-54bf-90fc-b8ea8a22c77d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1039 "Cases Where the Coefficient in the Absolute Value Is Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068718 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "65964b91-8f0e-5ffc-97d3-e5b87a6bade5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068719 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069135 #edb/ref 175